          %h2#BasicStructures The basic structures
          %p We discuss the setting in which fundamental physics takes place.
          %h3#Fundamentals Dynamics in space
          %p
            Physics is
            %em dynamics
            in
            %em spaces
            \.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+topos+theory"} Higher topos theory
            provides the formalizations of this most fundamental aspect of physics.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A general context for
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"}> space
                  s
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/big+topos"} big
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            %li
              %p
                A general context for
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} geometrical
                  spaces
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} local (∞,1)-topos
            %li
              %p
                A general context for geometrical spaces and
                %strong processes
                in these spaces is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            An example of relevance for much of physics is the cohesive
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9ELieGrpd"} ∞LieGrpd
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+groupoid"} ∞-Lie groupoid
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"}> smooth manifold
                s;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"}> orbifold
                s;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"}> diffeological space
                s;
            %li
              %p
                smooth
                = succeed "s;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+space"} path space
          %p
            In its
            = succeed "-version" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cahiers+topos"} Cahiers topos
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"}> infinitesimal space
                s;
            %li
              %p
                such as
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+space"}> de Rham space
                s, smooth
                = succeed "s;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-module"} D-module
          %p
            In its full
            = succeed "-version" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived geometry
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"}> derived ∞-Lie algebroid
              s such as the
              = succeed "es" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+complex"} BV-BRST complex
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
          %p
            Every
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            %strong kinematics
            in physics. Such as
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
            = succeed "s;" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+structure"} string structure
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fivebrane+structure"} fivebrane structure
          %p
            Every cohesive
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+%E2%88%9E-connected+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} locally ∞-connected (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} differential cohomology in an (∞,1)-topos
            classifying
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connections+on+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} connections on ∞-bundles
            %strong dynamics
            in physics: a connection on a principal
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            which exerts
            = succeed "s." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
          %ul
            %li
              the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+field"} Yang-Mills field
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
          %p
            In every such connected
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+class"} characteristic class
            gives rise to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+homomorphism"} ∞-Chern-Weil homomorphism
            that sends gauge fields encoded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connections+on+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} ∞-connections
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} principal ∞-bundles
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundles+with+connection"} circle n-bundles with connection
          %ul
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+circle+3-bundle"} Chern-Simons circle 3-bundle
              known from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+mechanism"} Green-Schwarz mechanism
              or the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+circle+7-bundle"} Chern-Simons circle 7-bundle
              known from
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+heterotic+string+theory"} dual heterotic string theory
          %p
            Under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport"} higher parallel transport
            of these circle
            = succeed "-bundles" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Simons+theory"} ∞-Chern-Simons theory
            of the corresponding characteristic class. This includes as special cases various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
            such as:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"}> Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
              , ordinary
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AKSZ+theory"} AKSZ theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BF-theory"} BF-theory
              coupled to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+Yang-Mills+theory"} topological Yang-Mills theory
              with cosmological constant.
          %p
            These are all
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theories"} topological quantum field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle+of+higher+category+theory"} holographic principle of higher category theory
            we have that on their boundaries live non-topological theories:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                on the boundary of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
                sits the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wess-Zumino-Witten+model"} Wess-Zumino-Witten model
            %li
              %p
                on the boundary of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
                sits the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
                dynamics of any classical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
                and on the boundary of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
                that of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
                phase space.
          %h3#quantum_dynamics Quantum dynamics
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            associated with such
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                on each closed piece
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mo −
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{d-n}
                of worldvolume
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
                of codimension
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-vector+space"} n-vector space
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            %li
              %p
                on each piece with boundary
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi in
                      %mo →
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ←
                      %mo ∂
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi out
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \Sigma_{in} \to \Sigma \leftarrow \partial \Sigma_{out}
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                between these
                = succeed "-vector" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p
                on each open subset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset \Sigma
                the
                %em
                  algebra of
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
                \.
          %p Two dual formalizations axiomatize this:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"}> AQFT
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra"}> factorization algebra
                s: the assignment of algebras of observables is encoded in an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-presheaf"} (∞,n)-copresheaf
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-algebra"} ∞-algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                with suitable properties;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"}> FQFT
                \: the assignment of spaces of states and propagators is encoded in an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-functor"} (∞,n)-functor
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} (∞,n)-category of cobordisms
                (see
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
          %p Well-understood examples of such quantum field theories include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"}> AQFT
                \:
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    2-dimensional
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
                    by
                    = succeed "s" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+net"} conformal net
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+chiral+homology"} topological chiral homology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"}> FQFT
                \:
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    2-dimensional
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
                    (see
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FFRS-formalism"} FFRS-formalism
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"}> TCFT
                    s (
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"}> (∞,1)-category
                    \-formulations of 2d
                    = succeed "):" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} homological mirror symmetry
          %h3#quantization Quantization
          %p By the above there is a fairly well-developed formalization of
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                background
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"}> quantum field theories
                \.
          %p
            The idea is that the former induce examples of the latter by a process called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            over the action functional.
          %p
            This step in full generality is not yet well understood formally. For a list of literature addressing this problem see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#LitOnQuantization"} Literature on quantization
          %p But special aspects of quantization are quite well understood. See for instance
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"}> geometric quantization
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+geometry"} multisymplectic geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf-algebraic+renormalization"} Hopf-algebraic renormalization
          %h2#the_detailed_structures The detailed structures
          %p
            We look at some aspects of the
            %a{:href => "#BasicStructures"} above
            general abstract story in more detail.
          %h3#GaugeTheory Gauge theory
          %h4#GaugeTheoryIntroduction Introduction
          %p
            The discovery of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            is effectively the discovery of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
            is close to synonymous to the notion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            and more generally to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} equivalence in an (∞,1)-category
            \.
          %p
            From a modern point of view, the mathematical model for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            in physics is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            in (nonabelian)
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            and its higher analogs. These naturally do not form just a set, but a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            and generally an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            has to a fair extent been motivated by and influenced by its application to fundamental theoretical physics in general and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            in particular.
          %p
            Around 1850 Maxwell realized that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            is modeled by what today we call a closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential 2-form
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            2-form of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+U%281%29-bundle"} principal U(1)-bundle
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            \.
          %p
            Meanwhile, in 1915, Einstein had identified also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} field of gravity
            as the
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔰𝔬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{so}(d,1)
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+group"} O(d,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
            on a
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d+1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+manifold"} Lorentzian manifold
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Weil+theory"} Chern-Weil theory
          %p
            In the 1950s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            identified the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            of all the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            as the
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{u}(n)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            2-forms of
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+group"} U(n)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
          .standout
            %p
              %strong
                Entities of ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              –
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
              with values in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              on
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
              over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            It is noteworthy that already in this mathematical formulation of experimentally well-confirmed fundamental physics the seed of higher differential cohomology is hidden: Dirac had not only identified the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            with connection, but he also correctly identified (rephrased in modern language) its underlying cohomological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
            with the (physically hypothetical but formally inevitable)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            located in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            is in fact
            %em not
            closed and hence in particular
            %em not
            the curvature 2-form of an ordinary connection on an ordinary bundle.
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Freed"}> Freed
            ) this old argument was improved by refining the model for the electromagnetic field one more step:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"} Dan Freed
            notices that the charge current 3-form is itself to be regarded as a curvature, but for a connection on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle 2-bundle with connection
            – also called a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} bundle gerbe
            – , which is a cocycle in degree 3
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            is fundamentally not quite a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+bundle"} twisted bundle
            with connection, with the twist being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            3-cocycle. Freed shows that this perspective is inevitable for understanding the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            is the presence of magnetic charge.
          %p
            In summary, the experimentally verified models, to date, of fundamental physics are based on the notion of (twisted)
            = succeed "-principal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(n)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+field"} Yang-Mills field
            and
            = succeed "-principal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(d,1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
          %p
            In attempts to better understand the structure of these two theories and their interrelation, theoretical physicists were led to consider variations and generalizations of them that are known as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            turns out to generalize: instead of just
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-algebra"} Lie 2-algebra
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-group"} Lie 2-group
          .standout
            %p
              %strong
                Entities of 2-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-algebra"} Lie 2-algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              with gauge
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-group"} Lie 2-group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              –
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+2-bundle"} connection on a 2-bundle
              with values in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              on
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundle"} principal 2-bundle
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} gerbe
              over an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Notably the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string
            is charged under a field called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            or
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              \-field
            which is modeled by a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundle"} principal 2-bundle
            with connection, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}U(1)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-group"} Lie 2-group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Lie+infinity-groupoid#BnU1"} circle Lie 2-group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-algebra"} Lie 2-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}\mathfrak{u}(1)
            is given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+crossed+module"} differential crossed module
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathfrak{u}(1) \to 0]
            which has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{u}(1)
            shifted up by one in homological degree.
          %p
            So far all these differential cocycles were known and understood mostly as concrete constructs, without making their abstract home in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            explicit. It is the next gauge field that made
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"} Freed
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Hopkins"} Hopkins
            propose (
            %a{:href => "#FreedHopkins"}> FreedHopkins
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Freed"} Freed
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
          %p
            The superstring is charged also under what is called the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            modeled by cocycles in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            form has coefficients in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra"} ∞-Lie algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %mn ∞
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                    %mi 𝔲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus_{n=0}^\infty \mathbf{B}^{2 n} \mathfrak{u}(1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b^{2n} \mathfrak{u}(1)
            is the abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E-algebra"} 2n-Lie algebra
            whose underlying complex is concentrated in degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 n
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          %p
            So fully generally, one finds
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra"} ∞-Lie algebra
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+group"} ∞-Lie group
          .standout
            %p
              %strong
                Entities of general
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra"} ∞-Lie algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              with gauge
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+group"} ∞-Lie group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              –
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+an+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} connection on an ∞-bundle
              with values in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              on
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
              over an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+groupoid"} ∞-Lie groupoid
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature+characteristic+form"} curvature characteristic form
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra"} ∞-Lie algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+theory"} ∞-Chern-Weil theory
            on nonabelian
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra"} ∞-Lie algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+Lie+2-algebra"} string Lie 2-algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+3-algebra"} supergravity Lie 3-algebra
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fivebrane+Lie+6-algebra"} fivebrane Lie 6-algebra
          %p
            Apart from generalizing the notion of gauge
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-group"} Lie 2-group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            that are more general than than the notion of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            is allowed to be more generally an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            or a generalization thereof, such as an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientifold"} orientifold
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stack"}> differentiable stack
              s
            \.
          %p
            It is noteworthy that the notions of generalized gauge groups and the generalized spacetime models encountered this way have a natural common context: all of these are examples of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} smooth ∞-groupoid
          %p
            There is a natural mathematical concept that serves to describe contexts of such
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} generalized space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gros+topos"} gros
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} differential cohomology in an (∞,1)-topos
            provides a unifying perspective on the mathematical structure encoding the generalized
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            models encountered in modern theoretical physics in such a general context.
          %h4#ClassesOfExamples Classes of examples
          %p
            We discuss classes of examples of gauge theories that have been considered. For all of these the configuration space is a space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connections+on+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} connections on ∞-bundles
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\nabla\}
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of sorts, which one might take to be the defining property of a gauge theory. But there are different types of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
          %h5#generalized_yangmills_theory Generalized Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            is of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ⋆
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nabla \mapsto \int_X \langle F_\nabla \wedge \star F_\nabla \rangle
                  \\,,
          %p where
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mo ∇
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\nabla
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            %li
              %p
                “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⋆
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star
                ” the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star"} Hodge star
                operator with respect to a fixed (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifold"}> pseudo
                \-)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"}> Riemannian metric
                \-structure on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -\rangle
                some
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
          %p
            This is the original notion of
            %em gauge theory
            and might be taken to be the strict sense of the term.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo =
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = U(1)
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
                this is
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            %li
              %p
                For gauge group a general nonabelian
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                this is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
                proper.
            %li
              %p
                Specifically for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                a discrete quotient of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SU
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi SU
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(3) \times SU(2) \times U(1)
                this is the gauge-field part of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            %li
              %p
                For structure group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo =
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = \mathbf{B}U(1)
                the
                %a{:href => "http://nlab.mathforge.org/nlab/show/Lie%20infinity-groupoid#BnU1"} circle 2-group
                this yields the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            %li
              %p
                For structure group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mn 2
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = \mathbf{B}^2U(1)
                the
                %a{:href => "http://nlab.mathforge.org/nlab/show/Lie%20infinity-groupoid#BnU1"} circle 3-group
                this yields the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            %li
              %p
                For structure group the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+spectrum"} K-theory spectrum
                we get
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
                describing the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            version of higher Yang-Mills theory – replacing a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+2-bundle"} connection on a 2-bundle
            is expected to control certain 6-dimensional theories that compactify to ordinary Yang-Mills on the torus and thereby explain
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"} S-duality
            \. See there for more details on this.
          %h5#chernsimons_theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-Chern-Simons theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Simons+theory"} ∞-Chern-Simons theory
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            is of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mi CS
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nabla \mapsto \int_X CS(\nabla)
                  \\,,
          %ul
            %li
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CS
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CS(-)
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+element"} Chern-Simons element
              for an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle - \rangle
              on an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
          %p
            This includes ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            in the case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+algebra"} semisimple Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            it subsumes a wide variety of types of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BF-theory"} BF-theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AKSZ+theory"} AKSZ theory
          %p
            The action functional of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Simons+theory"} ∞-Chern-Simons theory
            stands out by the fact that it arises by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} general abstract
            construction:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the underlying Lagrangian
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi CS
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∇
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CS(\nabla)
                is nothing but the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+homomorphism"} ∞-Chern-Weil homomorphism
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi CS
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi diff
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi n
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi diff
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      CS(-) : \mathbf{B}G_{diff} \to \mathbf{B}^n U(1)_{diff}
              %p
                induced by the given
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle - \rangle
                = succeed "-valued" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connections+on+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} connections on ∞-bundles
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle n-bundle with connection
                whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport"} higher parallel transport
                is given by the Lagrangian;
            %li
              %p
                the integral over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi CS
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∇
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CS(\nabla)
                is induced by postcomposition with the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated"} trunction
                morphism
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi conn
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mi CS
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi n
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi diff
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mi n
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mi Σ
                      %msub
                        %mi τ
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mi dim
                          %mi Σ
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mi n
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mi dim
                          %mi Σ
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mathbf{H}(\Sigma, \mathbf{B}G_{conn})
                      \\stackrel{CS(-)}{\to}
                      \\mathbf{H}(\Sigma,\mathbf{B}^n U(1)_{diff})
                      \\simeq
                      \\infty Grpd(\Pi(\Sigma), B^n U(1))
                      \\stackrel{\int_\Sigma}{\to}
                      \\tau_{n-dim \Sigma}
                      \\infty Grpd(\Pi(\Sigma), B^n U(1)) \simeq B^{n-dim \Sigma} U(1)
                      \\,.
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+algebra"} semisimple Lie algebra
                equipped with its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+form"} Killing form
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
                we have that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi CS
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CS(-)
                is the ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+element"} Chern-Simons element
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi X
                      %mi CS
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∇
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_X CS(\nabla)
                the ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
                action functional. This may be understood as the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport"} higher parallel transport
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+circle+3-bundle"} Chern-Simons circle 3-bundle
                with connection.
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+algebra"} semisimple Lie algebra
                equipped with its degree 8
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
                this yields 7-dimensional, whose action functional may be understood as the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport"} higher parallel transport
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+circle+7-bundle"} Chern-Simons circle 7-bundle
                with connection.
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+Lie+n-algebroid"} symplectic Lie n-algebroid
                equipped with its canonical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                of degree
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+2
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mi CS
                        %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_X CS_\omega(-)
                is the actional functional of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AKSZ+theory"} AKSZ theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    for
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
                    this is the
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
                %li
                  %p
                    for
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo =
                          %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 2
                    this is the
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Courant+sigma-model"} Courant sigma-model
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-algebra"} Lie 2-algebra
                equipped with the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+form"} Killing form
                invariant polynomial,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi X
                      %mi CS
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_X CS(-)
                is the action functional of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BF-theory"} BF-theory
                coupled to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+Yang-Mills+theory"} topological Yang-Mills theory
                with a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
          %h5#gravity Gravity
          %p
            The configuration spaces of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            may be identified with spaces of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connections+on+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} connections on ∞-bundles
            with gauge group a variant of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+group"} Poincare group
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation of gravity
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifold"} pseudo-Riemannian manifold
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+group"} special orthogonal group
                = succeed "-component" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mi O
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S O
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+connection"} spin connection
            %li
              %p
                the translation group-component of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∇
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
                is called the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vielbein"} vielbein
          %p
            For the ordinary Poincare group this yields the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Palatini+action"} Palatini action
            expression for the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
          %p
            In contrast to the
            = succeed "-Chern-Simons" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
            there is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+action"} spectral action
            functional on the space of spectral triples. This we discuss in more detail
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "SpecStandModAndGravity"} below
          %p
            There are various higher
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+extension"} group extension
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                lifting
                = succeed "-connections" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mi O
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S O
                %a{:href => "http://nlab.mathforge.org/nlab/show/Lie%20infinity-groupoid#SmoothWhitehead"} smooth Whitehead tower
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo →
                      %mi Fivebrane
                      %mo →
                      %mi String
                      %mo →
                      %mi Spin
                      %mo →
                      %mi S
                      %mi O
                      %mo →
                      %mi O
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\cdots \to Fivebrane \to String \to Spin \to S O \to O
              %p yields, in order of appearance,
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"}> spin structure
                    s and
                    = succeed "-" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+group"} spin group
                    = succeed "s" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
                  %p
                    This lift is necessary to cancel the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
                    of spinning particles coupled to gravity;
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+structure"}> string structure
                    s and
                    = succeed "-" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+2-group"} string 2-group
                    = succeed "s" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundle"} principal 2-bundle
                  %p
                    with
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+2-bundle"} 2-connection
                  %p
                    This lift is necessary to cancel the quantum anomaly of heterotic super
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"}> string
                    s;
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fivebrane+structure"}> fivebrane structure
                    s and
                    = succeed "-" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fivebrane+6-group"} fivebrane 6-group
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal 6-bundle
                    with
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+an+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} 6-connection
              %p
                This lift is necessary to cancel the quantum anomaly of
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} super 5-brane
            %li
              %p
                lifting to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+group"} super Lie group
                extensions of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SO
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO
                yields action functionals for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%27Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity"} D'Auria-Fre formulation of supergravity
                explicitly describes higher dimensional supergravity theories as gauge theories with higher super Lie structure groups
              %ul
                %li
                  11-dimensional supergravity is a gauge theory for the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+Lie+6-algebra"} supergravity Lie 6-algebra
          %h3#phenomenological_models_the_standard_model_and_gravity Phenomenological models: the standard model and gravity
          %p Theoretical physics consists of two parts: theory and models, laws and initial conditions, axioms and phenomenology.
          %p
            For instance the theory called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            describes the classical dynamics of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
          %p
            The theory that describes the fundamental forces and particles except gravity is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            The total collection of these choices of fundamental particles that are observed in experiments is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a choice of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
                on a
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            %li
              %p
                a choice of linear
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ρ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
                fields are components of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ρ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundle"} associated bundle
          %p
            What precisely the “standard” model of particle physics is changes slightly over time, as new experimental insights are gained. Its particles were added item-by-item as they were discovered. More recently the mass of the particles called
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutrino"} neutrino
          %p
            The standard model as far as understood today exhibits a curious mixture of pattern and irregularity. This seems to suggest that it ought to have a more fundamental description in terms of a conceptually simpler structure out of which these patterns with their irregularities emerge. Since also the force of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            is not presently included in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of the standard model, it may seem plausible that this underlying structure is related to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
          %p We discuss in the following some of the proposals that have been suggested for how to formalize this situation.
          %h4#SpecStandModAndGravity Spectral standard model and gravity
          %p
            A fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+particle"} relativistic particle
            is technically a 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            on 1-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"}> dimension
            al
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that it propagates in. Since both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            as well as
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+field"} Yang-Mills field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections
            it is plausible to assume that the only background field on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that the particle couples to is a connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_\rho
            on a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundle"} associated bundle
            over a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
          %p Here
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                roughly every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+algebra"} semisimple Lie algebra
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} summand
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo =
                      %mi Lie
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} = Lie(G)
                is one
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
                – a bosonic field;
            %li
              %p
                every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representation"} irreducible representation
                that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ρ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
                decomposes into is one matter particle species – a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinors+in+Yang-Mills+theory"} fermion field
          %p
            This way a single
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superparticle"} superparticle
            with different
            %em excitations
            or
            %em modes
            \.
          %p
            An early proposal for a single unified connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            that would subsume both gravity as well as Yang-Mills forces in a phenomenologically realistic way is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} Kaluza-Klein mechanism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Levi-Civita+connection"} Levi-Civita connection
            but on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifold"} pseudo-Riemannian manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            which is locally of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_4 \times F_{d}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_4
            a 4-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"}> dimension
            al pseudo-Riemannian manifold and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_d
            a
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            of very small Riemannian volume. As described in more detail at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} Kaluza-Klein mechanism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometry
            groups of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_d
            appear as extra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            factors as seen on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_4
          %p
            It was observed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"} Alain Connes
            and collaborators that the Kaluza-Klein mechanism works better when used not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            but in context for more general
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes-Lott-Chamseddine+model"} Connes-Lott-Chamseddine model
          %p
            This more general geometry turns out to model exactly the most general
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"}> Hilbert
                ) space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            %li
              %p
                An
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \hookrightarrow \mathcal{H}
                whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} multiplication operation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes A \to A
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+product+expansion"} operator product
                assigned to the trivalent interaction vertex
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mo •
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↘
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo •
                            %mtd
                              %mo →
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↗
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mo •
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      \\bullet
                      \\\
                      \&amp; \searrow
                      \\\
                      \&amp;&amp; \bullet &amp; \to
                      \\\
                      \&amp; \nearrow
                      \\\
                      \\bullet
                      }
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dyson+formula"} Dyson formula
                exponential
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mn 2
                      %mo +
                      %mi θ
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(t D^2 + \theta D)
                is assigned to a piece of 1-dimensional cobordism of superlength
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi t
                        %mo ,
                        %mi θ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t, \theta)
          %p
            This data is that of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
            %em spectral geometry
            ). It is therefore natural to search for a Kaluza-Klein ansatz in spectral geometry that would produce the standard model context.
          %p
            A very detailed such construction was given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"} Alain Connes
            (see the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FunctorialSpectral"} references below
          %p
            It turns out that the realistic model has
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mn 4
                    %mo +
                    %mn 6
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = 4+6
          %p
            By the result at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%281%2C1%29-dimensional+Euclidean+field+theories+and+K-theory"} (1,1)-dimensional Euclidean field theories and K-theory
            this
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superparticle"} superparticle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                  %mo +
                  %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = 4+6
            is precisely the dimension for which the 2-dimensional super-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            is critical and hence allows to lift the 1-dimensional superparticle described here to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %p
            This means that Connes’
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
            whose particle spectrum reproduces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            has a chance of being the point particle limit or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorification
            of the kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-spectral+triple"} 2-spectral triple
            – a 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} superconformal field theory
            – of the kind that is considered in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of 2-spectral triples called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
            might provide, via the second quantization of the latter, a (perturbative) quantization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+action"} spectral action
            of the former.
          %h2#RelatedIntroductions Related introductions
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+structures"} higher structures
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles+in+physics"} fiber bundles in physics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivation+for+sheaves%2C+cohomology+and+higher+stacks"} motivation for sheaves, cohomology and higher stacks
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+FAQ"} string theory FAQ
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivation+for+higher+differential+geometry"} motivation for higher differential geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/applications+of+%28higher%29+category+theory"} applications of (higher) category theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivation+for+cohesion"} motivation for cohesion
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert%27s+sixth+problem"} Hilbert's sixth problem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motives+in+physics"} motives in physics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory+and+physics"} model theory and physics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebras+in+physics"} L-infinity algebras in physics
          %h2#references References
          %p We list various references related to higher category theory and fundamental physics.
          %p
            A discussion of an axiomatization of basic concepts of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            is in
          %ul#SchreiberShulman
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Quantum+gauge+field+theory+in+Cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} Quantum gauge field theory in Cohesive homotopy type theory
          %p
            For general (formal) accounts of physics see also the references at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/books+and+reviews+in+mathematical+physics"} books and reviews in mathematical physics
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+Paugam"}> Frédéric Paugam
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://people.math.jussieu.fr/~fpaugam/documents/enseignement/master-mathematical-physics.pdf"} Towards the mathematics of quantum field theory
          %h3#CatsinPhysics Introductions to category theory in physics
          %p
            The translation between the axioms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+quantum+field+theory"} functorial quantum field theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            imagery is explained in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              , Section 1 of:
              %em Geometric Langlands From Six Dimensions
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0905.2720"}> arXiv:0905.2720
              )
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bob+Coecke"}> Bob Coecke
                ,
                %em Introducing categories to the practicing physicist
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0808.1032"}> arXiv:0808.1032
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bob+Coecke"}> Bob Coecke
                ,
                %em Categories for the practising physicist
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0905.3010"}> arXiv:0905.3010
                )
          %p
            the authors try to motivate and introduce some basic concepts of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            for an audience familiar with standard physics and in particular with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics+in+terms+of+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum mechanics in terms of dagger-compact categories
          %p
            More details on the use of string diagrams in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger-categories"} dagger-categories
            for the description of quantum mechanics is is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bob+Coecke"}> Bob Coecke
                ,
                %em Kindergarten quantum mechanics
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0510032"}> arXiv:quant-ph/0510032
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bob+Coecke"}> Bob Coecke
                ,
                %em Quantum Picturalism
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1787"}> arXiv:0908.1787
                )
          %p
            A similar introduction to the relation between quantum mechanics and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              %em Quantum quandaries
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0404040"}> arXiv:quant-ph/0404040
              )
          %h3#introductions_to_higher_category_theory_and_physics Introductions to higher category theory and physics
          %p
            A historical introduction to some aspects of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-categories"} n-categories
            (for low
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aaron+Lauda"} Aaron Lauda
              %em
                A prehistory of
                = succeed "-categorical" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              (
              %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/history.pdf"}> pdf
              ), to appear in
              = succeed "," do
                %em Deep Beauty: Mathematical Innovation and the Search for an Underlying Intelligibility of the Quantum World
          %p
            A historical introduction to applications in physics of more
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theoretic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            (revolving around
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"}> Jim Stasheff
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+Survey+of+Cohomological+Physics"} A Survey of Cohomological Physics
          %p The following book-to-be aims to give picture of the present state of the art of describing the category-theoretic structure of the universe, as far as fundamental physics is concerned
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"}> Hisham Sati
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              (eds.)
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory"} Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Field and Perturbative String Theory
          %p
            Amplifying the ambition towards
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert%27s+sixth+problem"} Hilbert's sixth problem
          %ul
            %li#MartinsBiezuner20
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuri+Ximenes+Martins"}> Yuri Ximenes Martins
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rodney+Josu%C3%A9+Biezuner"} Rodney Josué Biezuner
              = succeed "," do
                %em Towards An Approach to Hilbert’s Sixth Problem: A Brief Review
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-02909681"} hal:02909681
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-02909681/document"} pdf
          %h3#monographs_on_cohomology_and_higher_gauge_theory Monographs on cohomology and higher gauge theory
          %p An unusually comprehensive collection of detailed discussion of bundles, higher bundles, cohomology, characteristic classes, etc. with an eye on applications in physics is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dale+Husem%C3%B6ller"}> Dale Husemöller
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Joachim"} Michael Joachim
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Branislav+Jurco"} Branislav Jurco
              %em Basic bundle theory and K-cohomology invariants
              , Springer Lecture Notes in Physics 726, 2008 (
              %a{:href => "http://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~schotten/Texte/978-3-540-74955-4_Book_LNP726.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %h3#on_formal_quantum_field_theory On formal quantum field theory
          %p
            There are two axiomatizations of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#FQFTReferences"} FQFT references
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#AQFTReferences"} AQFT references
          %h4#FQFTReferences FQFT
          %p
            Kontsevich’s 1994 ICM article, is one of the seminal papers of the 1990s. This paper invented the categorical side of mirror symmetry (homological mirror symmetry), discovered D-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"}> brane
            s (before physicists realized their role — and directly inspiring many physicists) and the fact that they naturally form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg-categories
            and
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity-category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            in close relation with mathematical physics.
          %p
            In particular the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} Moore–Segal paper
            should be seen in the light of this development. On a similar note, roughly contemporary with Moore–Segal (which is
            %a{:href => "http://www.arxiv.org/abs/math/0609042"} arXiv:math/0609042
            though developed earlier) are the works of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"} Kevin Costello
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            (
            %a{:href => "http://www.arxiv.org/abs/math/0412149"}> arXiv:math/0412149
            ) and Kontsevich–Soibelman (
            %a{:href => "http://www.arxiv.org/abs/math/0606241"} arXiv:math/0606241
            — some of the results were lectured on in various places by Kontsevich in 2003 and in particular helped inspire Costello) proving a much stronger result, which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            (or equivalently differential graded or
            = succeed "-)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} homological mirror symmetry
            and topological
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+topology"} string topology
            which you mention and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} Hopkins-Lurie
            started from Costello’s paper and abstracted the argument, before the general argument in n-dimensions came around).
          %blockquote
            %p
              (in parts taken from
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/07/a_prehistory_of_ncategorical_p_1.html#c025075"} here
          %p
            For the moment see the references at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            for more.
          %h5#FunctorialSpectral On functorial spectral geometry
          %p
            Literature on the relation between
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
          %p The first proposal for such compactifications, which already came very close to the standard model, is decades old by now.
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Lott"} John Lott
              %em Particle Models And Noncommutative Geometry
              Nucl.Phys.Proc.Suppl.18B:29-47,1991 (
              %a{:href => "http://www.slac.stanford.edu/spires/find/hep/www?rawcmd=find+author+Connes+and+author+Lott&FORMAT=WWW&SEQUENCE="}> spires
              )
          %p This is reviewed in
          %ul
            %li
              Daniel Kastler, Thomas Schucker,
              %em The Standard Model a la Connes-Lott
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9412185"} arXiv:hep-th/9412185
              \.
          %p As far as I can tell, at that time the Yang-Mills terms were stilll included by hand. The main point of the spectral approach was to realize that it could nicely explain the Higgs boson and its Yukawa coupling terms to the fermions. It did (and does) so by realizing the Higgs boson as an internal part of an ordinary minimally coupled connection 1-form - an internal gauge boson.
          %p
            A few years later Connes apparently realized that also the gravitational and gauge kinetic Yang-Mills terms had an inherent operator-theoretic formulation, namely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+action"} spectral action
            principle.
          %p This is indicated in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              ,
              %em Gravity coupled with matter and foundation of non-commutative geometry
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9603053"} arXiv:hep-th/9603053
          %p and fully formulated in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ali+Chamseddine"}> Ali Chamseddine
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"} Alain Connes
              %em The Spectral Action Principle
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9606001"} arXiv:hep-th/9606001
          %p both of which start by presenting the general spectral idea and then working out how to realize the standard model in detail.
          %p A useful discussion of the details of the realization of the standard model in this approach is
          %ul
            %li
              C. P. Martin, Jose M. Gracia-Bondia, Joseph C. Varilly,
              %em The Standard Model as a noncommutative geometry: the low energy regime
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9605001"}> arXiv:hep-th/9605001
              ) .
          %p This is in particular designed to take the ordinary physicst by the hand and introduce him or her gently to the operator-theoretic spectral description by motivating these by the structure of the standard model.
          %p So already over ten years ago people had a pretty good idea that and how the standard model action has an elegant descritption as a spectral action.
          %p The only problem was: this description was wrong. But only in the sense in which ideas in physics tend to be wrong - not entirely wrong but not quite right.
          %p Namely, instead of containing the fermionic particle content of the standard model, these spectral models produced four copies of every fermion in the standard model. A slight overkill.
          %p By a remarkable synchronicity, it seems that there was no progress on this aspect for about ten years, and now two preprints appear almost simultaneously, presenting the solution:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Barrett"}> John Barrett
                ,
                %em A Lorentzian version of the non-commutative geometry of the standard model of particle physics
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0608221"} arXiv:hep-th/0608221
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
                ,
                %em Noncommutative Geometry and the standard model with neutrino mixing
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0608226"} arXiv:hep-th/0608226
          %p
            And the modification needed to get this solution is rather tiny, just a small change in the real structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            of the spectral triple from ten years ago.
          %p A survey is also at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em Connes on spectral geometry of the standard model IV
              (
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/09/connes_on_spectral_geometry_of_3.html"}> blog entry
              )
          %h4#AQFTReferences AQFT
          %p
            For the moment see the references at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            for more.
          %h3#LitOnQuantization On the quantization procedure
          %p
            First sketches of the idea that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            may have a formalization in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            appeared in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Algebraic+Structures+and+Quantization"} Higher Algebraic Structures and Quantization
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9212115"}> arXiv:hep-th/9212115
              )
          %p and its companion
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
              ,
              %em Quantum groups and path integrals
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9501025"}> arXiv:q-alg/9501025
              )
          %p More formal aspects along these lines appear in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Willerton"} Simon Willerton
              %em The twisted Drinfeld double of a finite group via gerbes and finite groupoids
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0503266"}> arXiv:math/0503266
              )
          %p
            An indication of a full formalization of what that may mean for discrete QFTs such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Hopkins"} Mike Hopkins
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Constantin+Teleman"} Constantin Teleman
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topological+Quantum+Field+Theories+from+Compact+Lie+Groups"} Topological Quantum Field Theories from Compact Lie Groups
          %p based on work in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} On the Classification of Topological Field Theories
          %h3#on_gauge_fields_and_differential_cohomology On gauge fields and differential cohomology
          %p
            The observation that generally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            is the correct formalism for describing
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
          %ul#FreedHopkins
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Hopkins"} Mike Hopkins
              %em On Ramond-Ramond fields and K-theory
              %p
                Journal of High Energy Physics 0005 (2000) 044 (
                %a{:href => "http://web.me.com/teichner/Math/Smooth_Cohomology_files/Freed-Hopkins.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p Motivated by this, the theory of differential cohomology itself was developed in
          %ul#HopkinsSinger
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Hopkins"}> Mike Hopkins
              , I. Singer
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quadratic+Functions+in+Geometry%2C+Topology%2Cand+M-Theory"} Quadratic Functions in Geometry, Topology,and M-Theory
          %p
            and shown to encode subtle
            = succeed "-cancellation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
          %ul#Freed
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization+and+generalized+differential+cohomology"} Dirac charge quantization and generalized differential cohomology
          %p
            This article starts with an introduction on basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            and points out that already there, in the presence of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomalies"} quantum anomalies
            shows that there are higher gauge theoretic effects even in this familiar theory.
          %h3#RefsOnToposTheoreticPhysics On topos-theoretic formulations of physics
          %h4#gros_toposes Gros toposes
          %p
            The observation that any formalization of physics ought to take place in a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            has been promoted early on by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Bill Lawvere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+mechanics"} continuum mechanics
            and his search for a formalization of physics made him end up being one of the most general abstract thinkers in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %p
            Lawvere thought about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical physics
            governed, as it is since Newton, by the theory of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            is something that takes place inside a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            – a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            with a suitable notion of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal object
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"}> Bill Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Categorical dynamics
          %p which is recalled in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"}> Bill Lawvere
              ,
              %em Toposes of laws of motion
              , transcript of a talk in Montreal, Sept. 1997 (
              %a{:href => "http://www.acsu.buffalo.edu/~wlawvere/ToposMotion.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            he sketched an outline both of synthetic differenial geometry in general and its application to the formulation of the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
          %p
            In this spirit he to search for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theoretic
            formalizations of fundamental (
            %em metaphysical
            in the good sense of the word) ontological concepts. A collection of such formalizations is indicated in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"}> Bill Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Taking categories seriously
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/reprints/articles/8/tr8.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            On
            %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/8/tr8.pdf#page=14"} page 14
            there appears the hint of the general abstract definition of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+topos"} locally connected topos
            as a general abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gros+topos"} gros topos
            context for geometric spaces. This idea was then refined to the concept of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"}> Bill Lawvere
              ,
              %em Axiomatic cohesion
              Theory and Applications of Categories, Vol. 19, No. 3, 2007, pp. 41–49. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/19/3/19-03.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            In this spirit a generalization of formalization of physics in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            is discussed in
          %ul#SchreiberLectures
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+a+cohesive+topos"} differential cohomology in a cohesive topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+smooth+cohomology+in+string+theory"} twisted smooth cohomology in string theory
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://maths-old.anu.edu.au/esi/2012/"} ESI program on K-theory and quantum fields
                (2012)
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Quantum+gauge+field+theory+in+Cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} Quantum gauge field theory in Cohesive homotopy type theory
              (2012)
          %h4#petit_toposes Petit toposes
          %p
            Independent of these developments is another proposal for a role of topos theory in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            initiated by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Isham"} Chris Isham
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kochen-Specker+theorem"} Kochen-Specker theorem
            – which may be used to formalize the way in which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            fails to secretly a theory of classical mechanics – has a natural interpretation in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+topos"} presheaf topos
            on the category of commutative sub-algebras of a given bounded
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebra
          %p
            This observation led Isham to propose a fundamental role of these presheaf toposes in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %ul
            %li
              Andread Doering, C.J. Isham,
              %em A Topos Foundation for Theories of Physics
              I-IV (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0703060"}> arXiv
              )
          %p This idea was further refined in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Heunen"}> Chris Heunen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolaas+Landsman"} Nicolaas Landsman
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bas+Spitters"} Bas Spitters
                %em A topos for algebraic quantum theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0709.4364"}> arXiv:0709.4364
                )
              %p
                %strong Abstract
              %p
                The aim of this paper is to relate
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} algebraic quantum mechanics
                to
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
                and quantum spaces. Motivated by Bohr’s idea that the empirical content of quantum physics is accessible only through classical physics, we show how a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star-algebra"} C-star-algebra
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                induces a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(A)
                in which the amalgamation of all of its commutative subalgebras comprises a single commutative
                = succeed "-algebra." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+duality"} Gelfand duality
                theorem of Banaschewski and Mulvey, the latter has an internal spectrum
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(A)
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(A)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
                of the system. Thus we associate a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
                (which is the topos-theoretical notion of a space and which intrinsically carries the intuitionistic logical structure of a
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                become
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measure"} probability measure
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(A)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                define continuous functions (more precisely, locale maps) from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(A)
                to Scott’s interval domain. Noting that open subsets of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(A)
                correspond to propositions about the system, the pairing map that assigns a (generalized)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth value
                to a state and a proposition assumes an extremely simple categorical form. Formulated in this way, the quantum theory defined by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is essentially turned into a classical theory, internal to the topos
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(A)
          %p
            A refinement of this from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            to
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joost+Nuiten"}> Joost Nuiten
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/bachelor+thesis+Nuiten"} Boohrification of local nets of observables
              \.
          %p
            See
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            for more.
          %h3#InPhenomenology In phenomenology and experimental physics
          %p
            In the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            is currently applied mostly for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
            in the context of 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            (modelling phenomena in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            (modelling critical surface phenomena in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+physics"} statistical physics
          %p
            Specifically 2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+quantum+field+theory"} extended quantum field theory
            has been applied to the solid state physics modeled by the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Levin-Wen+model"} Levin-Wen model
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexei+Kitaev"}> Alexei Kitaev
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liang+Kong"} Liang Kong
              %em Levin-Wen models and tensor categories ([pdf])
          %p
            For more discussion of 2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            in terms of higher category theory see the references there and at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRS+formalism"} FRS formalism
          %p
            In
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 3
            the
            %span.newWikiWord
              quantum hall effect
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/quantum+hall+effect"} ?
            and related effects are described to some extent by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+TQFT"} extended TQFT
            description of Chern-Simons theory down to the point, hence in terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-functors"} (infinity,3)-functors
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "Topological+Quantum+Field+Theories+from+Compact+Lie+Groups#3dCSFullyExtended"} here
          %p
            Closely related to this, several approaches at realizing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computing"} quantum computing
            rely on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            methods and are treated with methods from higher category theory. For instance the notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/blob+n-category"} blob n-category
            orginates in investigations of quantum computing.
          %p
            Indeed, if one counts computing as “experimental” or “phenomenology” – at least as related to the tangible world – then category theory is ubiquituous, see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+trinitarianism"} computational trinitarianism
            for the general idea and further pointers. For instance the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
            (see there) which underlies the formal understanding of modern
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+science"} computer science
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+2-categories"} equivalence of 2-categories
            in
            = succeed "." do
